{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n68 \n \n \nAnother parameter on GMT 340 was varying the ethylene concentration; while on GMT 349, we varied \nthe chamber pressure instead. On GMT 340, we used the ceramic fibers for measuring flame temperature; \nwhereas on GMT 349, we focused on determining sooting limits to better understand the conditions \nwhich cause soot to form or not. On GMT 347, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 \nto support continuation of Flame Design test points. \n \nCAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG \nIn this test point video, we translated the 14-micron ceramic fibers into the flame to optically \nmeasure the temperature through a technique known as Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP). The color \nof the fiber\u2019s glow is indicative of the temperature, where yellow is hotter than orange, which in \nturn is hotter than red. Due to excessive radiative heat loss, this large flame locally quenches and \nretreats from the burner supply tube (right-hand side). This hole allows the fuel and oxygen to \ndirectly mix, which enhances flammability and causes the hole to momentarily close. The cycle of \npartial quenching and recovery repeats with the oscillations growing in strength until the flame \ncompletely extinguishes. \n \nWeek 10: Dec. 17-23 \nOn GMT 355, Raja Chari replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support \ncontinuation of Flame Design test points. \n \nWeek 11: Dec. 24-30 \nOn GMT 361, we ignited 20 inverse flames for ACME Flame Design. We performed these tests with two \ndifferent ethylene concentrations with a range of oxidizer concentrations and flow rates from the burner. \nThe purpose of these tests was to determine at what conditions soot starts forming and to make detailed \nmeasurements of the flame at quasi-steady conditions (i.e., changes are occurring at such a slow rate that \nthe flame can be considered constant). \n \n \nFigure 1. Effects of Oxidizer Flow Rate on Flame Design Inverse Flames", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 68}}